# The new account's front door: hostname routing only. TLS is terminated by
# the ALB in front of this instance, not by Caddy — there is no Let's
# Encrypt HTTP-01 challenge here and no port 80 exposed to the internet
# (the security group admits the ALB's security group alone). This file is
# `:80 { ... }` throughout for exactly that reason: Caddy is never the TLS
# endpoint on this node.
#
# `/healthz` answers unconditionally so the ALB's target group health check
# does not depend on any one upstream service being up — see
# modules/app-node/user-data.sh.tftpl, which installs the same route as a
# placeholder before this file is ever deployed. Everything lives inside one
# `route` block rather than mixing a bare top-level directive with `handle`:
# Caddy sorts directives by its own internal precedence, not file order, and
# a bare `respond` alongside `handle` blocks lost to the catch-all `handle {}`
# in practice — `/healthz` came back 404 until this was a `route`, which
# evaluates its matchers top-to-bottom exactly as written.
#
# Every upstream is on 127.0.0.1 because Caddy runs with `--network host`,
# as do the services. Neo4j is reached the same way, over loopback — it is
# bound to 127.0.0.1 on the instance and is not reachable from Docker's
# bridge network at all.

# The ALB is the only thing that ever connects to this node, so the address
# Caddy sees as the peer is always the load balancer, never a caller. Declaring
# the load balancer trusted lets `{client_ip}` resolve past it to the address
# the ALB itself observed — the real client. Without this the placeholder
# returns the load balancer's own private address and every caller looks like
# the same one.
#
# This is measured rather than argued, against `caddy:2`, the image this node
# runs. A request arriving as `X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.99`:
#
#     plain reverse_proxy:                XFF="172.17.0.1"
#     with the ALB's ranges trusted:      XFF="203.0.113.99, 172.17.0.1"
#
# So `reverse_proxy` does NOT append to an inbound chain unless the immediate
# peer is a trusted proxy — it REPLACES the header with the peer's own address.
# Until this block existed the API had never seen a client address in
# production, only the load balancer's, and no TRUSTED_PROXY_HOP_COUNT could
# have recovered one.
#
# ## Why the list is the ALB's subnets and NOT `private_ranges`
#
# This block said `trusted_proxies static private_ranges` until a review caught
# what that means on a PUBLIC load balancer, and it is the same defect the
# application side already fixed one layer up: a trust list that can contain the
# caller is not a trust list.
#
# `private_ranges` is every RFC1918 block. The ALB is internet-facing, so a
# request can reach it from an RFC1918 source — a workload inside this VPC, a
# peered VPC, a VPN or Direct Connect client. The ALB appends the address it
# saw, so that private CLIENT address lands in `X-Forwarded-For`. Strict mode
# then walks from the right skipping every entry it considers a trusted proxy,
# classifies the caller's own private address as one, and keeps walking LEFT
# into the prefix the caller wrote. The caller therefore chooses `{client_ip}`,
# which this file writes into `X-Oxagen-Client-Ip` and `X-Forwarded-For` as
# though the edge had vouched for it, and which the application feeds into the
# IAM `ip_ranges` / `ip_allow` conditions — and those ALLOW on a CIDR match.
# Padding the header is free; nothing downstream can tell the difference.
#
# Note that the security group is not the mitigation. It bounds who can open a
# TCP connection to THIS listener, which is the ALB and only the ALB. It says
# nothing about who can reach the ALB, and the entry being trusted here is one
# the ALB wrote on behalf of somebody else.
#
# So trust the load balancer's actual address ranges. `__ALB_SUBNET_CIDRS__` is
# substituted at upload time by `infra/tools/install-node-scripts.sh` with the
# CIDR of every subnet the ALB is attached to, resolved from the live load
# balancer: `describe-load-balancers` gives its subnet ids and
# `describe-subnets` gives their CIDRs. Subnets are the right grain — the ALB's
# individual private IPs are per-AZ ENIs that come and go as it scales, so a
# list of addresses goes stale silently, while the subnets it scales WITHIN are
# fixed at `aws_lb.app.subnets` (`infra/stacks-new/oxagen/main.tf`, the two
# PUBLIC subnets from `module.network`). Re-running the installer refreshes the
# list, which is the only way this file is ever deployed.
#
# Those public subnets deliberately exclude the private subnets the workloads
# run in, so a VPC workload calling the public ALB is now correctly NOT a
# trusted proxy and the walk stops at its address.
#
# The placeholder has no fallback ON PURPOSE. It is not a valid CIDR, so an
# unsubstituted file fails `caddy validate` — which the installer runs on the
# node before the config is installed, and the node keeps the previous config
# when it fails — and the installer refuses to upload a rendered file that still
# contains it. A default would be the bug: every safe-looking default for "which
# proxies are in front of me" is a widening, and widening this list is exactly
# what the review found. Absent must fail, not fall back.
#
# ## Client address (ADR-083)
#
# Every application upstream below is handed the same client address twice, as
# `X-Oxagen-Client-Ip` and as `X-Forwarded-For`, and both are SET with
# `header_up` rather than appended to.
#
# The application reads `X-Oxagen-Client-Ip` (`extractTrustedClientIp`,
# packages/oxagen/src/client-ip.ts) — but only once an operator has set
# `TRUST_EDGE_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=true`, which is step 2 of the rollout and comes
# AFTER this file is uploaded and reloaded. The flag exists because the previous
# version of this file has no rule for that header name and forwards a
# caller-supplied copy straight through; a reader that trusted it on sight would
# be handed a forged address by name. Until the flag is set the application
# falls back to the `x-forwarded-for` walk, which is where it already was.
# ADR-083 carries the reasoning.
#
# `X-Forwarded-For` is rewritten to the same
# value because the chain itself was the vulnerability: the ALB appends and
# Caddy appends, neither strips an inbound copy, so the LEFTMOST entry reaching
# an upstream is whatever the caller typed. Anything reading that entry — and
# three surfaces did, feeding it into IAM `ip_ranges`, which ALLOWS on a CIDR
# match — could be handed an allowlisted address by the very caller it was
# meant to check.
#
# Rewriting it means no untrusted copy of that value exists inside the
# perimeter, so the next consumer who reaches for `X-Forwarded-For` out of
# habit is safe without knowing any of this. Nothing in this repo walks the
# chain: the four readers each want a single client address. A future consumer
# that genuinely needs the hops takes them from the ALB access logs, which keep
# the full chain (`access_logs` in stacks-new/oxagen/main.tf).
#
# `X-Real-Ip` is DELETED for the same reason. Nothing in this path sets it —
# not the ALB, not Caddy — so a value arriving under that name came from the
# caller, and it survived to the upstream untouched until this line. The
# application already refuses to read it; deleting it here means a consumer
# that reaches for it finds nothing rather than finding a lie.
#
# `trusted_proxies_strict` is what makes that address trustworthy rather than
# merely present. Caddy's default reads the LEFTMOST X-Forwarded-For entry,
# which is whatever the caller wrote, because the ALB appends to an inbound copy
# rather than stripping it. Strict mode walks the header from the right instead,
# skipping addresses inside the trusted ranges, so it lands on the entry the ALB
# appended and a caller-supplied prefix is ignored. Dropping this line does not
# fail any check; it silently hands `X-Oxagen-Client-Ip` to the caller.
#
# Strict mode and the list above are one mechanism, not two settings. The walk
# stops at the first entry that is not in the list, so the list decides where it
# stops — which is why widening the list back toward `private_ranges` does not
# merely coarsen the answer, it lets the caller pick it.
#
# ## After this deploys
#
# One application-side value finishes the rollout, and it is a deliberate act:
# `TRUST_EDGE_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=true` for apps/api, apps/app and apps/mcp, which
# is what makes `X-Oxagen-Client-Ip` believable. Until then the application
# ignores that header entirely and attributes NO client address at all. There is
# no hop-count walk to fall back to — that form was deleted in #3205, not
# deprecated — and the rewrite below leaves a single entry that is the client's
# own, with no proxy entry beside it for the identity walk to vouch with. So the
# window between this reload and that flag is briefly unattributed, and it fails
# closed: IAM `ip_ranges` mandates deny and the pre-authentication ceilings skip.
# That is the safe direction and the reason the flag is a separate step.
#
# Do NOT set `TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS` for this shape either, for the reason just
# given: it is the identity walk, and after the rewrite below there is no proxy
# entry left for it to vouch with. Setting it here attributes nothing rather
# than restoring anything. The variable belongs to the PRE-rewrite shape, where
# the ALB's own address is still in the chain. ADR-083's "The attribution order,
# stated once" has the sequencing; #3167 tracks the ceilings.
{
	servers {
		# Substituted by infra/tools/install-node-scripts.sh; see "Why the list
		# is the ALB's subnets" above. No default, deliberately — an
		# unsubstituted file must fail to parse rather than widen.
		trusted_proxies static __ALB_SUBNET_CIDRS__
		trusted_proxies_strict
	}
}

:80 {
	route {
		respond /healthz 200

		# --- Websites -------------------------------------------------------

		@stella host stella.oxagen.sh
		handle @stella {
			encode gzip zstd
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
		}

		@docs host docs.oxagen.sh
		handle @docs {
			encode gzip zstd
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3002
		}

		# --- Applications -----------------------------------------------------
		# These hold sessions and reach Aurora/ClickHouse/Neo4j over the network or
		# loopback respectively.

		# 300 s on app and api, matching the ALB's idle_timeout in
		# stacks-new/oxagen/main.tf. A model turn that answers in one buffered
		# reply can run past 60 s (a 64 s schema-chat turn on 2026-09-09), and
		# the first of the two proxies to give up turns a finished answer into
		# a 504. The handler bounds its own model call below this number.
		@app host app.oxagen.sh
		handle @app {
			encode gzip zstd
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
				# The client-address pair every application upstream gets; see
				# "Client address" in this file's header for why both are SET.
				header_up X-Oxagen-Client-Ip {client_ip}
				header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
				header_up -X-Real-Ip

				transport http {
					response_header_timeout 300s
				}
			}
		}

		@api host api.oxagen.sh
		handle @api {
			encode gzip zstd
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000 {
				# The client-address pair every application upstream gets; see
				# "Client address" in this file's header for why both are SET.
				header_up X-Oxagen-Client-Ip {client_ip}
				header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
				header_up -X-Real-Ip

				transport http {
					response_header_timeout 300s
				}
			}
		}

		@mcp host mcp.oxagen.sh
		handle @mcp {
			# Deliberately NOT encoded — see the old account's Caddyfile for why:
			# MCP's streamable HTTP transport is `text/event-stream`, and a
			# compressor in front of it batches events instead of streaming them.
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4100 {
				# The client-address pair every application upstream gets; see
				# "Client address" in this file's header for why both are SET.
				header_up X-Oxagen-Client-Ip {client_ip}
				header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
				header_up -X-Real-Ip

				flush_interval -1

				transport http {
					response_header_timeout 60s
					read_timeout 30m
					write_timeout 30m
				}
			}
		}

		# --- Internal ---------------------------------------------------------
		# Specs, pricing and plans; must not be publicly readable. The password
		# check is NOT here: it lives in the site's own Caddy container
		# (tools/internal-docs/Caddyfile), which reads the bcrypt hash from
		# Parameter Store at start. Putting it in this file would mean either
		# committing the hash or rendering it on the node, and a rendered file
		# breaks the boot path: user-data copies this file raw from S3, the
		# reload fails, and every hostname keeps serving 503.

		@internal host internal.oxagen.sh
		handle @internal {
			encode gzip zstd
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3003
		}

		respond 404
	}
}
